To validate an llms.txt file, check four things: it opens with an H1, every link resolves to the page its text describes, every package name and version exists on the registry, and nothing in it contradicts your current docs.


A docs tool probably generated your llms.txt during a launch sprint, and nobody has opened it since. Pages have been renamed and packages deprecated in the meantime, and coding agents keep reading the file anyway.


If you need a refresher on the format, start with[our llms.txt explainer](https://blog.openreplay.com/llms-txt-new-way-ai-read-site/) . This article skips the basics. It walks through the checks, gives you a zero-dependency Node script, and shows how to run it in CI so problems are caught before an agent acts on them.


## Key Takeaways


- The original llms.txt proposal requires only one element, an H1 naming the project or site. The blockquote summary, detail paragraphs and H2 link sections are all optional.
- The llms.txt proposal does not publish an official validator, and some online checkers flag a missing blockquote or H2 section even though the proposal treats both as optional.
- A package name in llms.txt that no longer exists on the registry can be registered by anyone. An agent with permission to run install commands may then pull in whatever they publish.
- Run the check on every deploy to catch mistakes you introduce, and on a weekly schedule to catch links and packages that break upstream without any change on your side.


## Why Is a Stale llms.txt a Supply-Chain Problem?


A stale llms.txt is a supply-chain problem because agents treat llms.txt as trusted reference material and often act on it with no human in between. That turns a stale link or a wrong package name into an instruction. A human reader who hits a dead link gives up. An agent with shell access may run` npm install` on a name that someone else now owns.


The llms.txt supply-chain risk has been demonstrated.[TechRadar reported research by Alon Hertz](https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/top-ai-tools-including-claude-codex-and-hermes-installed-suspicious-code-inside-corporate-networks) , who scanned 6,214 live domains and found 8,265 llms.txt and llms-full.txt files. By his count, the files on 120 separate sites pointed to packages or domains that nobody had registered. He then claimed some of those free names and published packages that reported back to him whenever someone installed them. According to the researcher, one Fortune 500 company’s systems started calling in within an hour. The report names Claude, Codex and Hermes as agents involved.


Keep this in proportion. It was a controlled experiment, not a record of malware in the wild. It is still a real supply-chain exposure, and a CI job closes it cheaply.


Access control is a separate concern.[Blocking AI crawlers with robots.txt](https://blog.openreplay.com/ai-crawlers-block-robots-txt/) tells crawlers which parts of your site they may read. Validating llms.txt controls whether what they read is still correct.


## What to Check When You Validate llms.txt


A valid llms.txt has an H1 and correctly formatted links, and every link and package reference points to something that exists and matches its description. The llms.txt proposal does not publish an official validator. The checks below cover what online checkers test, plus registry checks for the packages the file names.


### Links Resolve


Every URL in llms.txt should return a 2xx status once redirects are followed. Treat 4xx, 5xx and DNS failures as errors. A domain that no longer resolves is the domain version of an unregistered package: anyone can buy it. Log redirects as well. A redirect that lands on the site root usually means the page was removed. Send` HEAD` first and fall back to` GET` , because some servers reject or mishandle` HEAD` .


### Links Return What They Claim


A 200 response proves a URL answers. It does not prove the page still says what the link text promises. Check that the` content-type` is HTML or Markdown as expected. Compare the page’s` <title>` or first heading with the link text, and look out for soft 404s, where an error page is served with a 200 status. This check is fuzzy, so run it as a periodic review rather than a build gate.


### Packages and Versions Exist on the Registry


Check registry existence for every package your llms.txt tells an agent to install. By hand,[npm view <name>](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v12/commands/npm-view/) shows what the registry holds for that package and errors out if there is no such package. In a script, fetch the package document from` registry.npmjs.org` . A name the registry doesn’t know is a name someone else can claim. For pinned versions, confirm the exact version appears in the package’s published version list. PyPI, crates.io and other ecosystems offer equivalent lookups.


### Markdown Structure Is Valid


The[original llms.txt proposal](https://www.answer.ai/posts/2024-09-03-llmstxt.html) makes only one section mandatory: an H1 naming the project or site. Apart from an optional byte-order mark, the file must open with` # Name` . The current[llms.txt spec](https://llmstxt.org/) says each H2 section holds a Markdown list, and every item in it must start with a` \[name\](url)` link. Notes after a colon are optional. Fail the build on a missing H1 or broken link syntax. Don’t fail it for a missing blockquote or H2. Some online checkers flag both anyway, so don’t treat those warnings as spec violations.


### The File Doesn’t Contradict Current Docs


Compare the set of URLs in llms.txt with your` sitemap.xml` or your docs build’s route manifest. Treat any URL that appears in llms.txt but not in the sitemap as suspect. Do the same for packages: every name in the file should match the` name` field of a` package.json` you publish, or a dependency you intend to recommend.


## A Zero-Dependency Node Script to Validate llms.txt


This script takes a URL or a local path. It checks the H1 and every absolute link, then looks up each package named in an install command on the npm registry. It uses Node’s[global fetch](https://nodejs.org/api/globals.html#fetch) , so it runs without any dependencies on a current LTS such as Node 24.


```text
// scripts/check-llms.mjs — usage: node scripts/check-llms.mjs <url-or-path>
import   {   readFile   }   from   '  node:fs/promises  '  ;


const   src   =   process  .  argv  [  2  ];
if (  !  src  ) {   console  .  error  (  '  usage: node check-llms.mjs <url-or-path>  '  );   process  .  exit  (  2  ); }
const   failures   =   [];


async   function   load  (  s  ) {
if (  !  /^  https  ?  :\/\/  /  .  test  (  s  ))   return   readFile  (  s  ,   '  utf8  '  );
const   res   =   await   fetch  (  s  );
if (  !  res  .  ok  ) {   console  .  error  (  `  ✗   ${  s  }   returned   ${  res  .  status  }`  );   process  .  exit  (  1  ); }
return   res  .  text  ();
}


async   function   checkLink  (  url  ) {
let   res  ;
try   {
res   =   await   fetch  (  url  , {   method  :   '  HEAD  '  ,   redirect  :   '  follow  '   });
}   catch   {
// some servers reset the connection on HEAD; fall through to GET
}
try   {
if (  !  res  ?.  ok  )   res   =   await   fetch  (  url  , {   redirect  :   '  follow  '   });   // some servers refuse HEAD
if (  res  .  status   >=   400  )   failures  .  push  (  `  link   ${  res  .  status  }  :   ${  url  }`  );
else if (  res  .  redirected  )   console  .  warn  (  `  ↪   ${  url  }   ->   ${  res  .  url  }`  );
}   catch   (  err  ) {
failures  .  push  (  `  link unreachable (  ${  err  .  cause  ?.  code   ??   err  .  message  }  ):   ${  url  }`  );
}
}


async   function   checkPackage  (  spec  ) {
const   [,   name  ,   version  ]   =   spec  .  match  (  /^(  @  ?  [  ^  @\s  ]  +  )(?:  @  (  \S  +  ))  ?  $/  )   ??   [];
if (  !  name  )   return  ;
const   res   =   await   fetch  (  `  https://registry.npmjs.org/  ${  name  .  replace  (  '  /  '  ,   '  %2F  '  )  }`  );
if (  res  .  status   ===   404  )   return   failures  .  push  (  `  npm: "  ${  name  }  " is not registered  `  );
if (  !  res  .  ok  )   return   failures  .  push  (  `  npm: registry returned   ${  res  .  status  }   for "  ${  name  }  "  `  );
const   doc   =   await   res  .  json  ();
if (  /^  \d  +  \.\d  +  \.\d  +  /  .  test  (  version   ??   ''  )   &&   !  doc  .  versions  ?.[  version  ])
failures  .  push  (  `  npm:   ${  name  }  @  ${  version  }   does not exist  `  );
}


const   text   =   await   load  (  src  );
if (  !  /^  # \S  /  .  test  (  text  .  trimStart  ()))   failures  .  push  (  '  structure: file does not start with an H1  '  );


const   links   =   new   Set  ([  ...  text  .  matchAll  (  /  \]\(  (  https  ?  :\/\/  [  ^  )\s  ]  +  )  \)  /  g  )].  map  ((  m  )   =>   m  [  1  ]));
const   specs   =   new   Set  ([  ...  text  .  matchAll  (  /\b(  npx  |  npm   (?:  install  |  i  |  add  )  |  pnpm add  |  yarn add  )  \s  +  ([  ^  \n`  ]  +  )/  g  )]
.  flatMap  (([,   cmd  ,   args  ])   =>   {
const   tokens   =   args  .  trim  ().  split  (  /  \s  +  /  ).  filter  ((  t  )   =>   !  t  .  startsWith  (  '  -  '  ));
return   cmd   ===   '  npx  '   ?   tokens  .  slice  (  0  ,   1  )   :   tokens  ;
}));


for (  const   url   of   links  )   await   checkLink  (  url  );
for (  const   spec   of   specs  )   await   checkPackage  (  spec  );


failures  .  forEach  ((  f  )   =>   console  .  error  (  `  ✗   ${  f  }`  ));
console  .  log  (  `${  links  .  size  }   links,   ${  specs  .  size  }   package refs,   ${  failures  .  length  }   failures  `  );
process  .  exit  (  failures  .  length   ?   1   :   0  );
```


How it works:


- ` load` reads the file from disk or over HTTP. If the llms.txt URL itself fails, the script stops immediately.
- ` checkLink` tries` HEAD` first and retries with` GET` if the` HEAD` request errors out or returns a non-OK status. Redirects are logged as warnings, and network-level failures such as an unresolvable domain are recorded as failures.
- ` checkPackage` looks up each name on the npm registry and flags names that don’t exist, plus exact versions that were never published.
- Requests run one at a time on purpose, to keep load on your servers and the registry low.
- Any failure makes the script exit with code 1.


It has known limits. Relative links are skipped. Prose that follows an install command on the same line can produce false positives. Version ranges are not checked, only exact versions.


A failing run looks something like this (illustrative output with made-up names):


```text
↪ https://docs.example.com/guide -> https://docs.example.com/
✗ link unreachable (ENOTFOUND): https://docs.old.example.com/api
✗ npm: "example-sdk-legacy" is not registered
42 links, 3 package refs, 2 failures
```


## Run the Check in CI on Every Deploy and Every Week


Run the llms.txt validator twice over: once on every deploy against the file you just built, and once on a schedule against the live file. The deploy run catches mistakes you introduce. The scheduled run catches link rot and packages unpublished upstream while your repo stays untouched.


```text
name  :   Validate llms.txt
on  :
push  :
branches  : [  main  ]
schedule  :
-   cron  :   '  0 6 * * 1  '     # Mondays 06:00: catches link rot and unpublished packages
workflow_dispatch  :


jobs  :
check  :
runs-on  :   ubuntu-latest
steps  :
-   uses  :   actions/checkout@v7
-   uses  :   actions/setup-node@v7
with  :
node-version  :   24
-   run  :   node scripts/check-llms.mjs https://docs.example.com/llms.txt
```


The` schedule` trigger takes a standard five-field cron string, and[GitHub reads it as UTC](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax) unless you add a` timezone` key with an IANA zone name.` workflow_dispatch` lets you trigger a run by hand. If your docs deploy lives in the same workflow, add a step before the publish step that points at the build output, for example` node scripts/check-llms.mjs dist/llms.txt` , so a failing check blocks the release. Leave the scheduled job pointed at the live URL.


## Keep llms.txt in Sync by Generating It


If you generate llms.txt from the same source as your docs, the file can’t list a page or package the docs no longer contain. Build it in the same step that renders the docs, from the same page list and frontmatter:


```text
// scripts/build-llms.mjs
import   {   writeFile   }   from   '  node:fs/promises  '  ;
import   pages   from   '  ../dist/pages.json  '   with   {   type  :   '  json  '   };


const   lines   =   [  '  # Example SDK  '  ,   ''  ,   '  ## Docs  '  ,   ''  ];
for (  const   p   of   pages  .  filter  ((  p  )   =>   !  p  .  draft  )) {
lines  .  push  (  `  - [  ${  p  .  title  }  ](https://docs.example.com  ${  p  .  path  }  ):   ${  p  .  description  }`  );
}
await   writeFile  (  '  dist/llms.txt  '  ,   lines  .  join  (  '  \n  '  )   +   '  \n  '  );
```


Never hand-edit the generated file. Either commit only the generator, or commit both the generator and its output and have CI fail when regenerating produces a diff. Once generation is in place, the validator acts as a guard on the generator instead of a cleanup job someone has to remember.


## Conclusion


An llms.txt file only helps if everything it lists still exists and still matches its description, because agents act on it without checking. Add` scripts/check-llms.mjs` to your repo, run it once against your live file, fix whatever it reports, and then commit the workflow so the next broken link or orphaned package name fails a build before an agent finds it.


## FAQs


Should llms-full.txt be validated the same way as llms.txt?


Yes for links and packages, and more strictly for packages. llms-full.txt inlines full documentation content, so it usually holds far more install commands and code samples than llms.txt, and every one is a package name an agent may run. Structure checks can be looser, because the file is mostly page content rather than a short list of links. Point the same script at both files.


Why does the link check report 403 errors for pages that load fine in a browser?


Bot protection or a web application firewall is probably blocking the automated request. Some CDNs and security layers reject requests that lack browser-like headers or come from CI runner IP ranges. Allowlist your CI runner or the checker's User-Agent for your own docs domain, or send a descriptive User-Agent header with fetch. Don't simply ignore 403s, since agents may be blocked the same way.


How do I check Python package names from llms.txt against PyPI?


Query the PyPI JSON API at pypi.org/pypi/NAME/json. For an existing project it returns metadata plus a releases object keyed by version string, so you can confirm both the name and any pinned version. If the request returns a 404, treat the name as unregistered. Extract names from pip install and uv add commands the same way the script handles npm install.


Does a passing registry check mean a package in llms.txt is safe to install?


No. The check confirms that the name exists, not that the right people own it. Someone may already have claimed a name after the original package was abandoned, and a typo in llms.txt can point at a look-alike package that does exist. For every package you recommend, check that the publisher and repository link match your organisation, and prefer scoped names you control.


